Z||000000||Primaris||08-25-2001||12:05 AM||||Ok I just installed 4 sets of Carbomet pads and 4 cross-drilled rotors (new) from the Fiero Store.  I also drained / refilled the master cylinder, and rebuilt the rear calipers.  I bled the brakes at all four calipers.  I am not getting any stopping power, pedal travel has increased by about 2 inches, and the emergency brake has no holding power.  What the heck happened??  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Signatures are a waste of bandwidth!||||reg||1
Z||000001||mrfiero||08-25-2001||12:34 AM||||Sinc eyou say you rebuilt the rear calipers and have no e-brake anymore I would say your problem lies with the rear calipers.<P>I have never tried rebuilding calipers.....I figure it's worth the $$ to buy rebuilt ones....but maybe you didn't put the seal on right or ??  I really have no idea if that's even possible, but check your rear calipers for something amiss.<P>Good luck.    ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Primaris||08-25-2001||12:48 AM||||I've rebuilt a fair amt of calipers and these were pretty idiot proof.  I don't think it is a problem with the rebuild.  I think I'd be loosing fluid if the seals weren't installed properly.  What I don't understand is the increased pedal travel.  If anything with new pads the travel should be reduced.  Thanks for the input.<P>------------------<BR>Signatures are a waste of bandwidth!||||reg||1
Z||000003||DRH||08-25-2001||01:11 AM||||I've heard a misadjusted emergency brake will cause excessive pedal travel. You might want to try to adjust the emergency brake.||||reg||3
Z||000004||Raydar||08-25-2001||10:07 AM||||Once you get the caliper thing sorted out, what you'll probably find is a very hard pedal, but not enough "grab" to lock the wheels. <BR>That was my experience with Carbomet pads and new (but not cross-drilled) rotors. The pads are rock hard and don't work well until they're really hot. Once they break in, they'll be a little better, but still a hard pedal.<BR>Good luck.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Primaris||08-25-2001||12:44 PM||||Well I'll check into the e-brake adjustment.  I'm gonna rebleed the brakes but I not getting a spongy pedal.  Just a lot more travel.  I gotta fix this because I can't heel and toe with the brake this close to the floor.<P>------------------<BR>Signatures are a waste of bandwidth!||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||08-25-2001||01:04 PM||||Low, but solid, pedal is likely too much caliper clearance. Rear calipers on the 84-87s are great for being a $hit after service. I wouldn't be suprised if the 88s are the same.<P>read the cave update notice I just posted. I've no way to test it for 88 but I think it may work. You may need to alter the procedure slighty for an 88. You may need the shim to be a different shape or something like that. With a big clearance you may have to step on the brake pedal for step 8.<P>Make sure that the brakes do not drag after you try it. step on the pedal a couple times and then see how the wheel turns. A very slight drag of the outter pad is common but it should not impeed rotation. After that drive it around the block, no mor than a mile, and check it again. <P>Please post the results if you do the procedure and what if anyhting you had to change.<P><p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 08-25-2001).]||||reg||1
