Z||000010||Primaris||08-28-2001||12:17 AM||||Well I didn't try the Orge's shim method and I do still have a low pedal.  I gotta get this fixed so I can heel and toe again.  I guess I'll go buy some tin snips and give the shim method a try.  I did find and fix a small problem.  I adjusted the balancer on the e-brake and noticed only the D\S caliper was getting tighter.  After close inspection I noticed the cable to the handle was too far though the balancer.  Apparently there is a metal band that pushes in a tiny finger and this finger holds the cable housing back about a half inch from the end of the balancer.  The metal band was missing from my balancer.  So I used a small hose clamp and tightened it enough to make the small finger catch the brake cable housing and hold it in place.  I thought this would solve the low pedal problem by allowing proper adjustment of the pistons when I used the e-brake but no luck.  Oh, one note when the suspension is un-weighted and your adjusting your e-brake it will loosen when you put the car back on the ground.  So you need to put in a few extra cranks on the balancer just enough to make the e brake levers on the calipers come off their stops.  When you put the car on the ground check and make sure the levers moved back to their stops.<P>------------------<BR>Signatures are a waste of bandwidth!||||reg||1
